      "publication_date": "2013-08-01",
      "abstract": "AbstractCharles Taylor's influential exposition of Hegel made the doctrine of expressivism of central importance and identified Herder as its exemplary historical advocate. The breadth and generality of Taylor's use of \u2018expressivism\u2019 have led the concept into some disrepute, but a more precise formulation of the doctrine as a theory of meaning can both demonstrate what is worthwhile and accurate in Taylor's account, and allow us a useful point of entry into Herder's multifaceted philosophy. A reconstruction of Herder's overall philosophical position, centred around a refined theory of what this paper labels \u2018Herderean expressivism\u2019, reveals a naturalistic, teleological metaphysics. This metaphysics fulfils the Hegelian aim of providing what Dieter Henrich has called a \u2018feedback loop\u2019 between ontology and epistemology. Exploring Herder's expressivism, therefore, helps further the case for his decisive impact on Hegel's philosophy. Herder's methodological naturalism, however, represents an obstacle to Hegel's absolute idealism.Keywords: J. G. HerderG. W. F. Hegelexpressivism Notes1 A helpful guide to the politics of these disputes can be found in Hacking, Historical Ontology, 121\u201340. Berlin, it should be noted, celebrates Herder's cosmopolitanism and humanism. Yet he believes what he calls Herder's \u2018pluralism\u2019 slides into cultural relativism and finds its \u2018consequences\u2019 in Romantic anti-rationalism and nationalism. See Berlin, Vico and Herder, 212\u20133.2 In this paper, I concentrate upon Hegel, although an analogous account could certainly be given for Schelling. For some details of the Herder-Schelling relation, see Beiser, German Idealism.3 There is a long scholarly tradition emphasizing the decisive influence of Herder upon Hegel. See Harris, Hegel's Development. What Hegel read of Herder is difficult to ascertain. Most importantly, we know he wrote a (now missing) review of the two editions of Herder's dialogue Gott: Einige Gespr\u00e4che [henceforth God] of 1787 and 1800. Hegel refers to several of Herder's writings on the Old Testament in his early theological essays and later lectures on religion, and comments upon his philosophy in the 1802/1803 Glauben und Wissen. There are abundant signs that he was familiar with several of Herder's central works, in particular his Vom Erkennen und Empfinden der Menschlichen Seele [henceforth On the Cognition] and his pioneering work in the philosophy of history, the Ideen zur Philosophie der Geschichte der Menscheit [henceforth Ideas]. For a detailed examination, see Forster, Hegel's Idea of a Phenomenology.4 This limited reconstruction of Herder's philosophy certainly omits some of the ideas which were most relevant to his philosophical successors, especially his \u2018hylozoism\u2019 or vitalism, which was crucial both for the idealists' epistemological questions and for the development of Naturphilosophie. For these topics, see Nisbet, Herder and Scientific Thought; Zammito, \u2018Epigenesis\u2019, 131\u201345.5 Hacking, Why Does Language Matter, 15\u201343, offers a good account of how this characterization applies to Hobbes, the Port Royal Logic, Locke and Berkeley, all representatives of the \u2018way of ideas\u2019.6 This characterization of \u2018crude empiricist designativism\u2019 is indebted to Rosen. Taylor's presentation of the object of expressivist attack agrees with these particulars. He explains how pre-expressivist theories involve an ontological division between semantic and non-semantic entities, with the former gaining their semantic properties through merely \u2018external relations\u2019 of reference to the latter. These relations are established \u2018subjectively\u2019, and therefore exist in the mind alone.7 Herder, unlike certain later romantics, does not regard the coalescence of form and matter as an a priori obstacle to successful translation. His own theory of translation contains several innovative methods for recapturing the tones and senses of original works. For a helpful account of Herder's theory, see Ulrich Gaier's commentary to Herder's Volkslieder, especially W3: 909\u201318. References to Herder's German texts are cited by \u2018W\u2019 followed by volume and page number, and taken from Herder, Werke. Translations of Herder's writings are from Philosophical Writings, henceforth cited by \u2018PW\u2019 and page number, and Selected Writings on Aesthetics, cited by \u2018Aesthetics\u2019 and page number. Translations from Gott: Einige Gespr\u00e4che are from God: Some Conversations.8 See Irmscher, \u2018Beobachtungen zur Funktion der Analogie im Denken Herders\u2019, 64\u201398, 65\u201375 for discussion.9 See Nisbet for an extended discussion.10 See Clark, \u2018Herder's Conception of \u201cKraft\u201d\u2019, 737\u201352, for details.11 As Marion Heinz points out, sensations [Empfindungen] in Herder refer both to a system of bodily states or low-grade forces of representation (in Leibnizian fashion) and to representations of the soul on the other mediated through the body. The latter, cognitive, capacity is inseparable from the former. See Heinz, \u2018Grundz\u00fcge von Herders Psychologie\u2019, 137\u201352, 142.12 Cf. Herder, PW, 210\u201311, One will never get to bottom of these forces [i.e. mental capacities] if one merely treats them superficially as ideas that dwell in the soul, or, worse still, separates them from one another as walled compartments and considers them individually in independence.13 The many subordinate forces of the soul such as imagination, foresight and memory must not be seen as separated off from one another and as separate powers, but as at bottom a single force that is \u2018understanding, intuition with inner consciousness\u2019 or \u2018inner activity that looks within itself, consciousness, apperception\u2019 PW, 210\u201311.14 See Beiser, The Fate of Reason, 145ff. In the 1775 draft of On the Cognition, Herder states that \u2018Psychology must at every step be physiology\u2019. In this essay, Herder wants to base all sensation, and by extension all thought, on one purely physiological manifestation of force, Haller's discovery of \u2018irritability\u2019 [Reizbarkeit]. Psychology is equally physiology since \u2018Sensation is only the aggregate of all obscure irritations, just as thought is the luminous aggregate of sensation.\u2019 Herder, PW, 197. In the same draft, Herder explains how whilst there is a continuity of laws in sensation and cognition, they are present in the latter in a \u2018higher equation\u2019 and the \u2018string-play of an irritation \u2026 now communicates itself and takes effect in an infinitely more mentalistic, higher way\u2019 PW, 201.15 Irmscher, 86. Cf. Aesthetics 359: \u2018Human cognition starts from the senses and from experience, and everything comes back to them.\u201916 Gaier, \u2018The problem\u2026\u2019, 301 goes so far as to say that the notion of \u2018harmonisation\u2019 or \u2018coherence\u2019 functions as a \u2018transcendental principle\u2019 for Herder.17 In stark contrast to Hume, Herder believes that our activities of observation, abstraction, combination, and inference are, though not infallible, inner necessities of thought which derive from divine necessity. They are activities standing in analogous relation to the organizational principles governing groupings of forces throughout the cosmos. Hence, Herder states that this necessary basis of thought is to the world of minds what equilibrium is to the world of bodies. As such, they possess a qualified universality; a being with finer senses would still think according to the same unique necessary divine \u2018laws\u2019. Cf. God, 149\u201350 and PW, 208.18 PW, 215: \u2018true cognition and good volition are just one sort of thing, a single force and efficacy of the soul.\u201919 See the discussion at W1:144.20 For further discussion, see Stern, \u2018Kant, Hegel, and the Place of the Subject\u2019.21 This meaning of subjectivism is at issue in the discussion of cultural absolutism versus relativism in Spencer, Herder's Political Thought, chap. 4. Spencer contends that Herder charts a sophisticated middle course she calls \u2018weak pluralism\u2019.22 This form of subjectivism, criticised earlier as an element of Taylor's expressivism, can nourish a version of (2), especially an aesthetic version: putatively \u2018objective\u2019 standards cannot do justice to the unique particularity of artworks given their role as expressions of the artist's irreducible subjectivity. Indeed, many commentators criticize this element of Taylor's interpretation precisely for its association of Herder with elements of later Romanticism. See Spencer, Herder's Political Thought, 42\u20133, and Dallmayr, \u2018Truth and Diversity\u2019, 101\u201324, 107\u20139. I agree with Spencer, however, that Dallmayr throws the baby out with the bathwater by rejecting the term expressivism altogether.23 Whilst correctly distancing Herder from scepticism and idealism (\u2018fully-fledged scepticism\u2019), Sikka agrees that for both Kant and Herder our fundamental concepts are \u2018subjective\u2019 in being inapplicable to \u2018things in themselves\u2019. Sikka, 205.24 I thank Raymond Geuss, Michael Forster, Gabriel Roberts and two anonymous referees for the journal for their helpful comments on various versions of this essay.",
